<title>Exponential growth and decay modeled by continuous dynamical systems</title>
<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref> </p>
<description>
Overview of exponential growth and decay in continuous time, modeled by a linear autonomous differential equation.
</description>

<section><title>Transcript:</title>
<p>A continuous dynamical system, let’s say with time represented by the variable t, and the state variable represented by x, so that x(t) is the state at time t, such a continuous dynamical system can be represented by an autonomous differential equation. Let’s say of the form dx/dt = f(x), where here the rule of the dynamical system is represented by the function f.</p>

<p>This is the continuous dynamical system, because x(t) evolves continuously in time – there are no discrete time steps like in a discrete dynamical system. We write it as dx/dt(t) = f(x(t)), but usually we’ll suppress that t, and use the simpler form dx/dt = f(x). What’s the simplest form for a function f(x)? Well, we could have f(x) just being a constant; in that case the differential equation would be dx/dt = C, a constant.</p>
<p>What’s a function whose derivative is constant? Well that would be the function x(t) = Ct. The derivative of this function x(t) is just C, so indeed dx/dt is equal to C. In fact, we could add any other number d, and the derivative of x would still be C. But let’s not look at this case, that’s too boring.</p>
<p>Instead let’s look at the next simplest form for f: We could let f(x) be a linear function, let’s say f(x) = rx, where here the parameter r is the growth rate, at least if positive. If r is negative, then the derivative dx/dt is negative, so x is decreasing. In that case, we call r the decay rate. So our linear differential equation, or linear continuous dynamical system, is dx/dt = rx, where we just plug this f into the differential equation.</p>
<p>Can we find the solution to this differential equation? The solution means finding a function x(t), where if we differentiate, meaning we get dx/dt, is the same thing as multiplication by the number r, meaning we get r x(t). If x(t) is a solution to this differential equation, it means that differentiation with respect to t must be the same thing as multiplication by r, so we require that these are the same.</p>
<p>So for what type of function is differentiating with respect to t, the same thing as multiplication by r? We can find this solution by guessing. For the exponential function, the function is its own derivative – so this means, for e^t, differentiation equals multiplication by 1. But we don’t want differentiation to be the same thing as multiplication by 1, we want it to be multiplication by r, so instead we could try the function, e^(rt).</p>
<p>In this case, by the chain rule, if we differentiate we get an r that comes down, and so we get r times e^(rt) and this is exactly the same as multiplication by r. So the function e^(rt) satisfies our differential equation. And in fact, if we let x(t) equal any constant multiple of e^(rt), where C is just a number, this x(t) will be the solution. Let’s check that differentiation is the same thing as multiplying by r. Well, if we take x, and differentiate, we get the derivative of x, (d/dt (Ce^(rt)), and by the chain rule, an r comes down, we get Cre^rt. On the other hand if we take x and we multiply by r, we get x, which is e^rt, times r. Differentiation is the same thing as multiplication by r, so indeed x(t) = C e^(rt) is a solution of the differential equation dx/dt = rx. The general solution of the linear dynamical solution dx/dt = rx is x(t)=Ce^(rt). By general solution, we mean that all solutions have this form. For every number C, we get a different solution, where we take that number C and multiply it by e^(rt). </p>
<p>Now let’s add the initial condition. Let’s say we demand that x(0) = some number. Let’s call that number x_0. So if x(t) is Ce^(rt), then we know we satisfied the differential equation, but we still need to satisfy the initial conditions, we need if we plug int=0, we need to get x_0. What value of C will match the initial conditions? That’s pretty simple, x(0) = Ce^(r 0), which is C e^0, or C times 1, equals C. And we need this to equal x_0. Since we’re free to choose what C is, any value of C will give a solution, we can just choose C = x_0. So that x(t) is whatever the number x_0 is, times e^(rt). Then we satisfy the initial conditions. So, this is the specific solution to the differential equation coupled with the initial conditions. We call x(t)=x_0e^(rt) the specific solution, because it takes into account both the differential equation and its initial conditions.</p>
<p>What happens to x(t) as t gets large? What’s the behavior of the system? If r is positive, then x(t) gets larger and larger as t increases. So if r is greater than 0, we get exponential growth. On the other hand, if r is less than 0, then x(t) decays towards 0 as t gets larger and larger, because we get e to the negative of a very large number, so it gets smaller and smaller. So we get exponential decay.</p>
<p>Let’s add a population of bacteria that was growing, where we represented the size of the population by b(t). We might have a differential equation of the form, db/dt is 0.8 b. Let’s say with initial conditions b(0), the initial population size is 7. Then our solution, our specific solution is b(t) = 7 e^(0.8t). Just using the general form here, then indeed b(0) does equal 7, and we satisfy the differential equation that db/dt = 0.8 b. And if we were to plot bacteria population size versus time, we would get some exponential function that looks something like this – where the initial condition is 7, and the population size just gets larger and larger as time increases. So this gives us exponential growth. Maybe a good model for the initial growth of a bacteria population, at least until its growth slows down because it runs out of food or space or something.</p>
<p>We could also have a negative rate of change, if for example we had disappearing rhinos, well let’s say h(t) is the population size at time t. In that case, the differential equation might look something like dh/dt = -0.1h, for some initial condition, let’s say 1000. In this case, our solution h(t) = 1000 e^(-0.1t). This solution exhibits exponential decay. As the rhino population size is turning towards 0, or extinction. In this case, the graph of the population size as a function of time looks something like this, with initial condition of 1000. Depending on whether we have positive or negative rate of change, we get exponential growth or exponential decay from our linear differential equation.</p>
</section>
